'''The King's Head''' is one of the oldest [[public house]]s with a [[stagecoach|coaching]] yard in the south of [lEngland. It is located in the Market Square, [[Aylesbury]], Buckinghamshire, and is a Grade II* Listed Building.<ref name="NHLE">{{National Heritage List for England| num=1160205 |desc=The Kings Head Hotel |grade=II* |accessdate=15 July 2017}}</ref>

The oldest part of the current structure of the building is of 15th-century design<ref name="NatTrust">[http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/main/w-kingshead National Trust] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100706090930/http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/main/w-kingshead |date=2010-07-06 }}</ref> and the cellars are much older, dating to the 13th century.<ref name="ReferenceA">Discover The King's Head, Aylesbury: The National Trust King's Head Guidebook 2009</ref>

==History==
The history of The King's Head starts in 1455,<ref name="NatTrust" /> with the first documentary reference appearing in a conveyance between William Wandeford, a London wool merchant, and Ralph Verney, a former master of the London Mercer's Company, dated 18 December 1455. It refers to the newly built 'Kyngeshede', as well as a cellar and shop, and cottages.<ref name="ReferenceA"/> The Great Hall is the oldest standing structure on site, dating back to the 1470s,{{cn|date=May 2026}}.
[[Image:Aylesbury KingsHeadInn01.JPG|thumb|left|Courtyard]]
[[Henry VI of England|King Henry VI]] possibly stayed at the inn while on a tour of the country with his new wife [[Margaret of Anjou]] in the 15th century.<ref name="BExp">[http://www.britainexpress.com/counties/bucks/houses/kings-head.htm Britain Express]</ref> Later, a [[stained glass]] panel, previously in the nearby [[Greyfriar]]s [[monastery]], was inserted in the front window of the inn showing the [[monarch|king]] and [[Monarch|queen]]'s individual [[coat of arms|coats of arms]].<ref name="BExp" /><ref>[http://www.british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=42520 British History Online]</ref><ref name="AVDC">{{Cite web |url=http://www.aylesburyvaledc.gov.uk/leisure-culture/local-history-heritage/aylesbury/sights-around-aylesbury/ |title=Aylesbury Vale District Council |access-date=21 June 2010 |archive-date=27 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110927181222/http://www.aylesburyvaledc.gov.uk/leisure-culture/local-history-heritage/aylesbury/sights-around-aylesbury/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In the Great Hall [[wattle and daub]] timber construction is visible<ref name="NatTrust" />

==The National Trust==
The property was donated to the [[National Trust]] in 1925, a fact commemorated by a plaque at the site.{{cn|date=May 2026}}

