[[File:LongCrendonCourthouse(DavidHawgood)Aug2005.jpg|thumb|Long Crendon Courthouse]]

'''Long Crendon Courthouse''' is a 15th-century two-storeyed [[timber frame]] building located in the village of [[Long Crendon]] near [[Aylesbury]], [[Buckinghamshire]], [[England]], and now a [[National Trust]] property and [[Grade II* listed building]].<ref>{{NHLE|num=1214040|desc=Long Crendon Courthouse|grade=II*|accessdate=14 May 2014}}</ref>

It is believed that the building was used as a [[wool]] store before serving to house [[manorial court]]s, which were held here from the reign of [[Henry V of England|Henry V]] until the reign of [[Victoria of the United Kingdom|Victoria]].

The ground floor (which is now let out as a flat) was the village [[poor house]].

